               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="120X/121X" notBefore="1200" notAfter="1219"
                        calendar="#gregorian">s. xiii in.</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_46">Beeleigh, Essex.
                        Premonstratensian abbey of St Mary the Virgin and St
                           Nicholas</orgName><p><q>ecclesie sancte Marie et sancti Nicholai De
                           Meldone</q> (on flyleaf at end, fol. 120, s. xiii).</p><p>Beeleigh was
                        called Maldon until the thirteenth cent. <q>De Meldone</q> in the
                        inscription is erased and not quite legible, but there can be no doubt about
                        the reading since <q>de Meldon'</q> is scribbled twice below. The
                        inscription is more probably a <q>probatio penne</q> than a formal ex
                        libris.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_1012">Milton (near
                        Canterbury), Kent. Parish church</orgName><p>This attribution was rejected
                        in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964), which
                        did not change in the Supplement (1987). The corresponding Medieval
                        Libraries of Great Britain index card shows that the attribution was based
                        on a misinterpretation of the inscription <q>ecclesie sancte Marie et sancti
                           Nicholai De Meldone</q>. The card now ostensibly serves to redirect the
                        reader, and includes a large later note: <q>This is from Beeleigh (=
                           Maldon)</q></p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><q><persName role="fmo">Wyllyam
                           Amys</persName></q> (s. xvi).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><q><persName role="fmo">Jacobus
                           Riddings</persName> vicarius de Penkrich iure habet hunc codicem</q> (s.
                     xvi/xvii).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">The numeral 21 inside a box on fol. v is a
                     17th-cent. Bodleian number. <q>Probably acquired between 1605 and
                     1614</q>.</provenance>
               </history>
